  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1: A return to a busier, stormier pattern tomorrow \nthrough the weekend. \n\nCurrently, mid and upper level ridging occupied the southern\nhalf of the CONUS, while faster westerly flow was located \nacross the northern half. This ridging was helping to keep us \nwarm and dry, but starts to break down as early as tomorrow as a\nband of westerlies shifts south from the Great Lakes/OH Valley \nregions. Accompanying this will be a surface cold front, which \nis expected to move into the Mid Atlantic region tomorrow \nevening.\n\nExcept for daily afternoon cumulus and some passing cirrus,\nskies have been clear today, allowing for deep mixing and \nmoderately drier conditions. Winds at the surface remain light \nand WSW through tomorrow. After that, a cold front approaches \nfrom the north tomorrow night, moving through WV and part of VA \nbefore stalling in VA and NC Friday. Over the weekend, this \nfeature will lift north as a warm front. \n\nAs far as thunderstorm chances, we are outlooked for general\nthunder by SPC Thursday through Sunday. We are also included \nin a Marginal Risk for excessive rainfall from WPC over the\nweekend given the slow-moving/stalling front along with high \nPWATs, so isolated flash flooding will be possible. Shear for \nstorm organization looks weak Thursday with the front still to \nthe north, and expect isolated and multi- cells interacting via \noutflows over SE WV. Isolated damaging winds will be possible \nwhere/if storms can briefly take advantage and become severe. \nShear and instability both improve Friday and Saturday, and \nthese look like better chances for damaging winds and also flash\nflooding, especially from the Blue Ridge westward. \n\nIncreasing warm air advection will allow temperatures to be\nabout 5 to 10 degrees above normal Thursday, resulting in \nreadings in the mid 80s to the mid 90s areawide. By Friday into \nthe weekend, abundant cloud cover will lower temperatures closer\nto normal, with highs generally in the mid 70s to low 80s for \nthe mountains, and the 80s for the Piedmont for highs. Overnight\nlows will also be about 4-6 degrees above normal with cloud \ncover helping to keep temperatures warm. Does not look like we \nwill be very close to breaking any records during this warm\nspell.\n\nThe relative humidity will also increase Friday through the\nweekend, making it muggier. For Monday into Tuesday, we look \nmostly dry, with a few showers/isolated storms possible for the \nsouthern Blue Ridge. The next frontal passage will occur the \nfollowing Wednesday.",
